body:
### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?

(Re-)assigning  `.fillStyle`/`.strokeStyle` is a performance bottleneck. Is there a faster way to change the color that does not incur large performance overhead? I couldn't find any clear solution in the spec or from other sources, apologies if I overlooked something.

If the 2D Canvas API doesn't facilitate this, please consider extending it; it seems like an unnecessary performance limitation.

## Background

I'm writing a small 2d collision detection simulation where a bunch of balls with semi-random **color** and radii bounce off one-another.

The `drawBalls` JS-function batch-renders the balls as circles each frame, in a loop. Here's the code:
```js
    drawBalls(structSize, arrayPointer, count) {
        canvasContext.clearRect(0, 0, canvasWidth, canvasHeight);
        for (var i = arrayPointer;
                 i < arrayPointer+structSize*count;
                 i += structSize
        ) {
            var ballX      = wasmMemoryInt16View[ i    / 2]/8;
            var ballY      = wasmMemoryInt16View[(i+2) / 2]/8;
            var ballRadius = wasmMemoryUint8View[ i+4 ];
            var ballColorR = wasmMemoryUint8View[ i+5 ];
            var ballColorG = wasmMemoryUint8View[ i+6 ];
            var ballColorB = wasmMemoryUint8View[ i+7 ];

            canvasContext.beginPath();
            var cssColorString = String.fromCharCode(
                '#'.charCodeAt(0),
                (ballColorR >> 4) + ((ballColorR >> 4) > 9)*7
                + '0'.charCodeAt(0),
                (ballColorR & 15) + ((ballColorR & 15) > 9)*7
                + '0'.charCodeAt(0),
                (ballColorG >> 4) + ((ballColorG >> 4) > 9)*7
                + '0'.charCodeAt(0),
                (ballColorG & 15) + ((ballColorG & 15) > 9)*7
                + '0'.charCodeAt(0),
                (ballColorB >> 4) + ((ballColorB >> 4) > 9)*7
                + '0'.charCodeAt(0),
                (ballColorB & 15) + ((ballColorB & 15) > 9)*7
                + '0'.charCodeAt(0));
            canvasContext.strokeStyle = cssColorString;
            cssColorString = null;
            canvasContext.lineWidth = 2*ballRadius;
            canvasContext.moveTo(ballX, ballY);
            canvasContext.lineTo(ballX, ballY);
            canvasContext.stroke();
        }
    }
```
And here's a screenshot of a time-slice of the performance using the dev-tools in Chrome version 140.0.7339.80 (representative of performance over the reset of the measurement window):

<img width="1157" height="1208" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/51406867-cd5a-4b3d-9bee-54de298ae9ec" />

As visible, the majority of CPU-time each frame is consumed by `drawBalls`. Looking at the line-by-line performance measurements for `drawBalls`, you can see that the `.strokeStyle = ...` assignment is the largest contributor to the slowdown:

<img width="830" height="837" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f9125572-63e3-4e17-84db-4732cd9cc1ca" />

cumulatively, 2010ms (sorry, it's a bit hard to see because of the background color), approx. 4x the time taken by the `.stroke()` call.

## Expectation

It was surprising that assigning a new color turned out to be a performance bottleneck. I tried to research an alternative and was hoping to find 2d-context methods similar to `.setStrokeColorRGB(<number>, <number>, <number>)/.setFillColorRGB(...)` or be able to directly set color-channel values:
```js
ctx.strokeColorR = 255;
ctx.strokeColorG =   0;
...
```
I think this is what many users of the Canvas API will expect that have past experience with other graphics APIs. You could argue that a string-value is more intuitive for beginners. This seems subjective without non-bias empirical evidence because contrary arguments can be made. For example, you could argue that numeric values leave less room for error than possibly-incorrect string values or that more prerequisite string manipulation knowledge is required to work with them.

More importantly, other applications could hit this unforeseen performance wall. In the worst case, Canvas API code would need to be re-written to use the WebGL or WebGPU APIs, costing a significant amount of time, possibly learning and future maintenance cost due to added complexity. 

I see that `CSSColorValue` objects are introduced in the CSS Typed OM spec. draft https://www.w3.org/TR/css-typed-om-1/#colorvalue-objects. I'm not sure to what degree this would solve the problem in the future because I'm unsure of the performance characteristics of the `CSSRGB` constructor. If this is guaranteed to make additionally imposed overhead negligible, then that's great! Otherwise; I think it's worth strongly considering additions to the API to allow colors to be changed without large overhead.
